From 094d6d98fb52378f03fe992d5fc9a5cb4a4f167d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cgwalters-bot <cgwalters-bot@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 20:41:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] sysroot: Read the booted boot filesystem from
 /sysroot/boot

When /boot is a directory of the physical root, the deployment's /boot is
just a bind mount of /sysroot/boot made by the boot.mount generator, and
other units can stack mounts on top of it. On a BIOS-booted system with an
unused ESP, systemd-gpt-auto-generator puts boot.automount for the ESP
there. Once that automount idles out, accessing /boot from another mount
namespace fails with EPERM, so e.g. `unshare -m ostree admin status`
fails with "fstatat(boot/loader): Operation not permitted".

On a booted system, open boot_fd from /sysroot/boot in that case; that
path has nothing stacked on it. With a separate boot partition, keep using
/boot. For that, the code that still went through "boot/..." relative to
sysroot_fd (reading the bootversion, preparing the new boot/loader link,
the bootswap entries directory, and cleaning up old loader directories and
initramfs overlays) now uses boot_fd too.

`finalize-staged --hold` relied on the sysroot's boot_fd to keep /boot
busy until shutdown. When that fd is for /sysroot/boot, also hold /boot
if it's the bind mount of it. Otherwise the automount expires, which
stops boot.mount and with it ostree-finalize-staged.service
(RequiresMountsFor=/boot), so finalization runs right away. Anything else
at /boot, such as an ESP, is left alone to expire.

Related: https://github.com/bootc-dev/bootc/issues/2402

Assisted-by: https://github.com/cgwalters/cgwalters#llms
